# 7. Authorization

## 7.1 Authorization States

Aura shall map HomeKit authorization into the following internal states:

```swift
enum AuraHomeAuthorizationState: Sendable, Equatable {
    case unknown
    case loading
    case authorized
    case restricted
    case denied
    case unavailable
}
```

The exact HomeKit framework status must remain available to diagnostics, but normal UI should use Aura-friendly language.

## 7.2 Permission Flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start[User opens Home integration] --> Check[Check authorization]
    Check -->|Authorized| Load[Load homes]
    Check -->|Undetermined| Explain[Show permission explanation]
    Explain --> Request[Trigger HomeKit access]
    Request --> Result{Result}
    Result -->|Allowed| Load
    Result -->|Denied| Denied[Show Settings guidance]
    Check -->|Denied or Restricted| Denied
    Load --> Ready[HomeKit ready]
```

Aura must explain why access is needed before triggering the system permission flow.

Suggested permission copy:

> Aura uses access to your Apple Home to show and control your rooms, lights and other smart-home accessories. Your home data stays on your devices and in Apple's HomeKit system.

## 7.3 Denied Access

When access is denied:

- Existing cached HomeKit data must not be presented as live.
- Aura shall show a clear explanation.
- The primary action shall open the relevant app settings where supported.
- The user must still be able to use non-HomeKit integrations.
- Aura shall not repeatedly trigger or simulate permission prompts.

